// The two marks the dashboard paints behind a card that has run out of quota: 危 for a maxed 5-hour
// window, 死 for a maxed 7-day one. They are the pool owner's own brush glyphs, so they cannot be
// synthesised from a font stack the way the rest of this page's typography is.
//
// CARRIED AS BASE64, IN THE BUNDLE, ON PURPOSE. Every alternative loses something the dashboard
// depends on: a /assets route would put two more entries in the wire contract and make the page
// stop rendering correctly the moment a proxy or a stale worker mismatched them; reading the files
// from disk at startup would tie the master to a layout that `bun run build` does not produce
// (dist/tui.js is a single file, and dist/ is gitignored); a remote URL would fail exactly when the
// page is needed most, on a master with no internet route — the same reason the page links no
// webfont. This keeps dashboardHtml() what its header promises: ONE string, zero dependencies.
//
// Downscaled from the 440px originals to 248px (sips -Z 248), which is 2x the 124px the mark is
// painted at, and painted at 0.13 opacity — the size below is what that costs, ~57KB of base64 for
// the pair. Do not "optimise" this by re-exporting them as a sprite or a shared glyph: the two are
// selected by MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE state classes (see .card.si beating .card.wei in dashboardHtml.ts)
// and must stay two independent images.

